// The teeth of P1: R1/R2 must survive the CALL BOUNDARY. A read-only rune passed
// where a writer is expected is rejected AT THE CALL SITE — without the direction
// in the type, this program would compile and write a read-only register.
//
// It also proves the printer: the diagnostic names both directions, where a
// direction-dropping printer would say "expected rune<u16>, got rune<u16>".
fn burst(r: write rune<u16>) { r.write(64); }

fn main() {
    rune status: u16 at 0xDFF002 read;
    burst(status);
}
